README Pacman

OVERVIEW:
This program creates the Pacman game. The PaneOrganizer class is the top level graphics
class and the Game class is the top level logic class. The App class models the PaneOrganizer class
and sets up the scene, which is added to the stage. An instance of the game class is called in the
PaneOrganizer class to instantiate the game. The game class handles any user interactions
and takes care of the overall logic of the game. There is a Pacman class that models and creates
the Pacman in the game, and also contains methods that allows pacman to move. It also contains
methods that relates to Pacman in the game. There is a Collidable inheritance class,
which is implemented by the Ghost, Energizer, and Dot class. Each of those classes models
and creates the respective collidables. Instances of these collidable classes are created
in the game class to create and model the four ghosts, and the energizers and dots found on the map.
The board is a 2D array that was created in the Game class by referencing the CS15SupportMap
and by using the Square class. The Square class is a wrapper class that created the squares
graphically and contained the list of the collidables on the square at any given moment.
Mode and Direction are enums that included the possible modes and possible directions.

DESIGN CHOICES:
The game utilizes several classes that all work together in different ways to make the
game perform the way it should. I used association to pass objects throughout the different
classes to ensure that they all knew about the same one. For examples, I would pass the
board from the game into the ghost class by passing it through a method called on
the ghosts in the game class. I also used getters and setters in the classes to
get the necessary variables or objects. For example, all of the collidables classes
and the pacman class had getRow and getCol methods that would return the row or
column that the collidable or pacman was on. Another example was the collidables
list that was created in the square class. I had a getList method that would return
the list when it was called on a square.

To create the map, I ran a double layer for loop to reference the CS15 support map that was
given to us. The double layer for loops models the rows and columns of the board, and
would create the collidables and pacman depending on where they are supposed to be
located on the board. I had to create the ghosts separately due to how the for loop
iterates. I added all the collidables on a square to the collidable list stored in the
square.

I would change the ghostModes based on several indicators. One was collision, where
if pacman ate the energizer, the game mode would change to frightened. Another was
the use of counters. I created a counter that would tell me when to set the mode
to chase or scatter, and another to tell me when to set the mode to frightened.

I wrote the breath first search algorithm in the ghost class since the ghosts
were the ones that needed the algorithm. The algorithm would be used for chase
and scatter modes but not for the frightened mode. For the frightened mode I had
a method that would return a random valid direction.

To see if the game was over I had a counter to see whether pacman had eaten all
the dots and energizers.

Bells and whistles:
- You can press D to change the speed of the game. Pacman and the ghosts will move faster.
Pressing D again will return the speed to the original speed.
- There are three ghost mode tokens at the start of the game. These tokens allow you to change the
ghost mode by pressing C for chase, S for scatter, and F for frightened. However, if you run out of
tokens, then a label will appear to tell you that you have no more tokens to use.

KNOWN BUGS: no known bugs

